<h2>Two frameworks, two logics</h2>
<p>The summary remedy under Section 144 of the BNSS exists to prevent destitution, so its disqualifications are narrow, exhaustive and strictly proved. The matrimonial-court remedies under the Hindu Marriage Act serve a wider adjustment of rights, so conduct enters as one factor in a discretionary balance. The practical consequence: a husband resisting a Section 144 claim must bring his case within a defined statutory ground, while a husband resisting permanent alimony argues conduct as part of the overall merits — and a wife answering either must know which logic is in play.</p>

<h2>The Section 144(4) grounds, precisely</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Ground</th><th>What must be proved</th><th>What does not suffice</th></tr>
<tr><td>Living in adultery</td><td>A continued adulterous course of life, as a settled state of affairs</td><td>A solitary lapse, suspicion, or unproved association</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refusal to live with the husband</td><td>Refusal <em>without sufficient reason</em> — the reason-side is part of the inquiry</td><td>Refusal explained by cruelty, dowry demands, expulsion, or the husband\'s second marriage or mistress, which the statute itself treats as just ground</td></tr>
<tr><td>Separation by mutual consent</td><td>A genuine consensual arrangement to live apart</td><td>Separation forced by circumstances, or acquiescence in an expulsion</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Two features of the scheme deserve emphasis. First, the grounds attach to the <em>wife\'s</em> allowance; the children\'s entitlement stands on its own footing and does not fall with a finding against the mother. Second, sub-section (5) makes the same grounds a basis for cancelling an existing order — so the disentitlement battle can be fought after judgment as well as before it, on fresh proof.</p>

<h2>Stage matters: interim versus final</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>Courts in Delhi consistently resist converting interim maintenance hearings into conduct trials. At the interim stage — Section 24 HMA, or interim relief within a Section 144 petition — the inquiry is a prima facie one into means and needs, worked through the affidavits of assets and liabilities. Serious disentitlement pleas, such as living in adultery, ordinarily require evidence and are decided at the final stage; at best they temper quantum in the interim. Respondents who front-load such pleas without material achieve little beyond hardening the record against themselves; claimants who face them should insist on the distinction of stages.</p>
</div>

<h2>Conduct under the Hindu Marriage Act</h2>
<p>Section 25 directs the court, in fixing permanent alimony, to have regard to the parties\' income and property, their conduct, and the other circumstances of the case. Conduct here is not a trapdoor but a dial: proven misconduct can reduce quantum, shape the form of the award (periodic rather than lump sum), or in egregious cases justify refusal, while unproven allegations are weightless. Section 24, by contrast, asks principally whether the applicant lacks independent income sufficient for support and for the costs of the proceedings — a deliberately low-friction gate designed to keep both spouses able to litigate.</p>

<h2>Working rules for both sides</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
<li>For respondents: plead the statutory ground by name, with particulars and material; a disentitlement defence that is actually a character attack fails and costs credibility.</li>
<li>For claimants: meet the reason-side head on — the record of cruelty, demands or expulsion that explains separate residence is the complete answer to a refusal-to-reside plea.</li>
<li>Keep the children\'s claim insulated: whatever the findings between the spouses, the children\'s maintenance survives them.</li>
<li>Remember the modification track: changed circumstances are argued under the alteration provisions, not disguised as disentitlement.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>Maintenance law\'s architecture is ultimately symmetrical: it refuses to let a dependent spouse be starved into submission, and it refuses to let the remedy be weaponised where the statute\'s own disqualifications are proved. Knowing exactly where those disqualifications begin — and where they end — is what separates a disciplined maintenance practice from an exchange of accusations.</p>
